!!<*yamlover: $defs: chapter>
One omnivorous node
description: scalar, list, and dict are not boxes — one node may be all three at once
- >
  yamlover has one node model, not three. A node may carry a scalar **self-value**, named (keyed)
  entries, and positional (keyless) entries — in any combination, at once. There is no
  scalar/list/dict kind to choose first and be locked into: the familiar kinds are just the corners a
  node happens to occupy while it exercises one capability.
- !!yo
  rating: 5      # the node's own scalar value …
    - solid      # position 0 … and a positional entry …
    scale: 10    # … and a keyed field — ONE node
- >
  The example above is **live**: the body element is marked `!!yo` — plain yamlover, exempt from the
  enclosing chapter schema — so the chapter view you are reading hands it to the generic yamlover
  renderer instead of interpreting it as a subchapter. This book writes every runnable example that
  way.
- >
  **Mixtures are the default.** An untagged node may mix keyed and keyless entries and may carry a
  self-value alongside fields — at the document root included. `!!mix` remains a no-op documentation
  marker; the `!!yo` tag is semantic — plain yamlover, exempt from the enclosing document's schema
  ([mixtures](::language:vs-yaml:mixtures)).
- >
  The payoff is stability under growth: adding a field to a node — an annotation onto a markdown
  string, a subchapter under a title — never flips its type out from under a consumer. A type is a
  predicate over a node's shape, and a node may satisfy several at once (the [logical
  model](::language:model)).
